---
const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/acoyfellow/flue-snippets';
---

<section class="hero">
  <h1>Flue 2.0 on Cloudflare</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    I wanted to see what it actually looks like to build agents with
    <a href="https://flueframework.com">Flue</a> on Cloudflare Workers. Turns out the answer
    is mostly: less code than I expected. Four agents I built, smallest to largest.
    Every snippet here is <strong>Flue 2.0</strong>: an agent is a function marked
    <code>'use agent'</code> that returns its instructions. Flue 2 removed workflows,
    so anything an agent should <em>do</em> is a tool it calls.
  </p>
  <p class="meta muted">
    Every snippet really deploys to a real Worker. Around $0.0001 per run, the e2e
    destroys the Worker after it asserts. Each deployed agent route is gated on a
    per-run <code>SNIPPET_API_KEY</code> (<code>x-api-key</code>), because these Workers
    bind Workers AI on a public URL.
    <span class="updated">Last dependency refresh: May 17, 2026, 3:07 AM PT.</span>
    <a href={repoUrl}>Repo →</a>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .meta {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .updated {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
